  {
    "title": "Why Hypothetical Leaderboard Metrics Fall Short for Local LLM Deployments Needing Low Latency & Offline Operation",
    "body": "This is a discussion based on a hypothetical experiment. When considering local LLM deployments, especially for a team prioritizing low latency and offline functionality, a purely leaderboard-driven selection process may prove insufficient. Leaderboard scores, while offering a standardized comparison, often abstract away the nuances of real-world operational constraints. The hypothetical scenario specifies a need for minimal response time and complete independence from network connectivity. These are distinct performance characteristics that typical leaderboard benchmarks may not adequately assess. For instance, a model with a high leaderboard score might still exhibit unacceptable latency on local hardware or require online components. Conversely, a model with a slightly lower general score might offer superior performance in a strictly offline, low-latency setting. It's crucial to remember that no specific model is being presented as superior in this hypothetical context; the point is the inadequacy of leaderboard scores alone for these particular requirements.",
